1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,628
That's a mission I wrote,

2
00:00:02,078 --> 00:00:04,425
starting on a computer that isn't mine.

3
00:00:05,325 --> 00:00:08,389
Today is the last lecture of this class,

4
00:00:08,839 --> 00:00:10,791
and there's nothing new to learn in it.

5
00:00:11,691 --> 00:00:13,880
Today, you simply finish it.

6
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:17,570
You make the mission yours,

7
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:19,834
you check it, you print it,

8
00:00:20,284 --> 00:00:22,177
and then you give it to someone.

9
00:00:23,377 --> 00:00:25,436
Don't start by typing.

10
00:00:26,336 --> 00:00:28,444
What you've built is a shape:

11
00:00:28,894 --> 00:00:32,035
go out, find a second thing, wait,

12
00:00:32,485 --> 00:00:34,956
and come home, against a clock.

13
00:00:35,856 --> 00:00:39,389
Every slot in that shape has a name that a player reads,

14
00:00:39,839 --> 00:00:41,834
and a handle that the game uses.

15
00:00:42,734 --> 00:00:45,124
So write your story into the slots first.

16
00:00:46,924 --> 00:00:50,218
Here's mine: a navigation beacon has gone dark,

17
00:00:50,668 --> 00:00:53,615
its keeper is missing, and a storm is ten minutes out.

18
00:00:54,815 --> 00:00:58,850
There's one rule: change names and sentences, and leave every key.

19
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:05,749
Every name and sentence I change is one the crew reads somewhere, so here's where.

20
00:01:06,649 --> 00:01:09,790
The Quest Log shows the arc's name, and every step's,

21
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:13,548
and those are the square brackets, which are mine.

22
00:01:14,448 --> 00:01:16,520
The round brackets are the game's.

23
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:20,420
So my beacon is still called derelict underneath,

24
00:01:20,870 --> 00:01:23,481
and no player will ever see that word.

25
00:01:25,281 --> 00:01:29,747
The end screen, Game results, shows a win sentence or a lose sentence,

26
00:01:30,197 --> 00:01:32,932
and those are these two lines.

27
00:01:33,832 --> 00:01:36,824
The order on the right of the Quest Log is an objective,

28
00:01:37,274 --> 00:01:40,844
and the number in it must still agree with the line under it.

29
00:01:41,744 --> 00:01:44,866
And Science shows a reading on the beacon's Scan tab,

30
00:01:45,316 --> 00:01:47,026
so I change that sentence too.

31
00:01:47,926 --> 00:01:49,787
The two briefings change the same way:

32
00:01:50,237 --> 00:01:53,963
each paragraph and each row of the table gets my words,

33
00:01:54,413 --> 00:01:56,416
and every mark stays where it is.

34
00:01:57,316 --> 00:02:00,768
That comes to sixty-six changes, and not one key.

35
00:02:02,568 --> 00:02:06,518
Then the script, which has five places, all between quote marks.

36
00:02:07,418 --> 00:02:10,450
The names Helm's map draws beside each ship

37
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:12,938
are the first pair of quotes on each line.

38
00:02:13,838 --> 00:02:17,654
The second pair is the side and the roles, so leave it.

39
00:02:18,854 --> 00:02:22,060
Lint says clean, because words can't break the chain,

40
00:02:22,510 --> 00:02:24,429
and that's why we change them first.

41
00:02:25,629 --> 00:02:27,811
Now, two files you haven't opened.

42
00:02:28,711 --> 00:02:32,119
This one is what the server shows in its list of missions,

43
00:02:32,569 --> 00:02:34,347
and you change two lines in it.

44
00:02:35,247 --> 00:02:39,137
And there's one rule here that matters more than any other today:

45
00:02:39,587 --> 00:02:42,910
no hyphen, and no colon, in these two values.

46
00:02:43,810 --> 00:02:48,147
The game reads this file for every mission, before it shows anything,

47
00:02:48,597 --> 00:02:50,231
and a hyphen here stops the game.

48
00:02:51,131 --> 00:02:53,185
Not your mission, the game.

49
00:02:54,985 --> 00:02:58,444
Lint reads this file now, and it stops you with an error.

50
00:02:59,344 --> 00:03:00,785
Doctor doesn't read it.

51
00:03:01,685 --> 00:03:04,932
So keep to plain words, commas and full stops,

52
00:03:05,382 --> 00:03:07,189
and run lint after you change it.

53
00:03:08,989 --> 00:03:11,499
Then the ship, on line seventy.

54
00:03:12,399 --> 00:03:16,374
Change the word between the quote marks, and nothing else.

55
00:03:17,274 --> 00:03:20,718
If I break this line, my ship is Artemis again, and nothing tells me.

56
00:03:21,618 --> 00:03:22,984
I find out when I play.

57
00:03:24,184 --> 00:03:26,590
You don't have to do this.

58
00:03:27,490 --> 00:03:29,578
But if a key bothers you,

59
00:03:30,028 --> 00:03:31,932
there's one way, and only one:

60
00:03:32,382 --> 00:03:35,990
Replace, across the whole folder, with both buttons on.

61
00:03:36,890 --> 00:03:39,500
Read the list first: there are nine places,

62
00:03:39,950 --> 00:03:43,658
and look at the last one, which is in the script, on my card.

63
00:03:44,558 --> 00:03:47,475
So it's replace all, save all, and lint.

64
00:03:47,925 --> 00:03:49,722
It's clean, and it works.

65
00:03:51,522 --> 00:03:53,115
Now for the other way.

66
00:03:54,015 --> 00:03:56,878
I change it by hand, all through the fact sheet.

67
00:03:57,778 --> 00:03:59,707
Lint says clean, and it's broken.

68
00:04:00,607 --> 00:04:03,903
The tender never comes, and the game can't be won.

69
00:04:04,804 --> 00:04:06,158
The address on the card

70
00:04:06,608 --> 00:04:09,729
is the one place lint can't follow a key.

71
00:04:10,929 --> 00:04:14,420
Everybody in this class has a folder called My Mission.

72
00:04:15,320 --> 00:04:17,499
So if you send yours to a classmate,

73
00:04:17,949 --> 00:04:19,522
it lands on top of theirs.

74
00:04:20,422 --> 00:04:23,450
That's why it gets its own name, with no spaces.

75
00:04:24,350 --> 00:04:27,303
Nothing inside the files knows the folder's name.

76
00:04:28,203 --> 00:04:31,079
Only I do, in every command from now on.

77
00:04:32,279 --> 00:04:33,464
There are three checks,

78
00:04:33,914 --> 00:04:36,246
and each one sees what the others can't.

79
00:04:37,146 --> 00:04:39,920
Lint asks, is the writing right?

80
00:04:40,820 --> 00:04:44,066
Doctor asks, is everything this mission needs on this computer?

81
00:04:44,966 --> 00:04:47,495
Look for the block with my folder's name,

82
00:04:47,945 --> 00:04:50,898
and then the last two words: zero problems.

83
00:04:51,798 --> 00:04:53,749
Doctor never reads my writing.

84
00:04:55,549 --> 00:04:57,138
And then comes the play.

85
00:04:58,038 --> 00:05:01,963
There's my ship, my station, and my beacon, on Helm's map.

86
00:05:02,863 --> 00:05:05,920
On Science, I pick Lantern Nine from the list,

87
00:05:06,370 --> 00:05:09,928
and its Scan tab shows one of my new lines in the read-out.

88
00:05:10,828 --> 00:05:13,537
Then the skiff, the wait, and the tender.

89
00:05:14,437 --> 00:05:17,465
Then home, and my win line on Game results.

90
00:05:18,365 --> 00:05:20,788
Both logs are empty, which is what I want.

91
00:05:21,688 --> 00:05:25,179
And then I read every sentence once more, as a reader.

92
00:05:26,379 --> 00:05:28,309
My mission is also a text,

93
00:05:28,759 --> 00:05:30,241
and it takes one command.

94
00:05:31,141 --> 00:05:35,902
It has a title, it says player, which means leave out the machinery,

95
00:05:36,352 --> 00:05:37,956
and it asks for a PDF.

96
00:05:38,856 --> 00:05:41,216
That makes two files, in a new folder.

97
00:05:43,016 --> 00:05:44,710
This is for a friend who reads,

98
00:05:45,160 --> 00:05:46,874
and not for a friend who plays,

99
00:05:47,324 --> 00:05:49,882
because it's the whole plot, in order.

100
00:05:50,782 --> 00:05:53,334
And there's one for me, called the bible.

101
00:05:54,234 --> 00:05:56,156
It goes beat one, beat two,

102
00:05:56,606 --> 00:05:58,650
with reached from, and leads to.

103
00:05:59,550 --> 00:06:01,234
If a step's in the wrong place here,

104
00:06:01,684 --> 00:06:04,241
a Then line points at the wrong place.

105
00:06:05,141 --> 00:06:07,003
There's one thing it gets wrong:

106
00:06:07,453 --> 00:06:10,627
it says win, false, and lose, false.

107
00:06:11,527 --> 00:06:13,327
My two sentences are fine.

108
00:06:13,777 --> 00:06:15,569
It's the printing that isn't.

109
00:06:16,769 --> 00:06:18,149
A mission is a folder,

110
00:06:18,599 --> 00:06:21,456
so to share it, I send the folder.

111
00:06:22,356 --> 00:06:24,857
First I take out what isn't the mission.

112
00:06:25,757 --> 00:06:28,484
That's my documents, because they're the plot,

113
00:06:28,934 --> 00:06:31,562
and the scratch folder and the logs,

114
00:06:32,012 --> 00:06:33,942
which come back by themselves.

115
00:06:34,842 --> 00:06:36,468
That leaves seven files,

116
00:06:36,918 --> 00:06:39,536
and it's all seven, not only the two I wrote.

117
00:06:40,436 --> 00:06:42,434
Then the folder itself gets zipped,

118
00:06:42,884 --> 00:06:44,872
and it comes to seven kilobytes.

119
00:06:46,072 --> 00:06:48,814
My friend puts the folder where mine is.

120
00:06:49,714 --> 00:06:51,903
There's one check: open it,

121
00:06:52,353 --> 00:06:55,495
and the files are right there, not another folder.

122
00:06:56,395 --> 00:06:59,206
Then it's four lines: is the tool new enough,

123
00:06:59,656 --> 00:07:01,222
and if it isn't, update it.

124
00:07:02,122 --> 00:07:05,302
Then fetch the libraries this mission names,

125
00:07:05,752 --> 00:07:09,855
because even if they play every week, theirs may be older than mine.

126
00:07:10,755 --> 00:07:11,720
And then start it.

127
00:07:12,920 --> 00:07:14,105
And that's the class.

128
00:07:15,005 --> 00:07:18,170
The rubric on the page is what done means,

129
00:07:18,620 --> 00:07:21,133
and you can tick every line yourself.

130
00:07:22,033 --> 00:07:23,775
Then do the first exercise:

131
00:07:24,225 --> 00:07:26,723
send it to one person, and don't help them.

132
00:07:28,523 --> 00:07:31,017
Class 2 puts people in your story.
